30 Minutes Human # 00051
Lad, Scavenger, Doll, Clean, Old Woman
September 30, 2025

The spider had turned into a messy smudge on the top of the pipe. The young man checked the sole of his boot, found it mostly spider-free, and continued on his way.
He lightly hopped around the rocks and flagstones, raincoat swinging to and fro because he'd failed—not forgotten, but opted not—to fasten it. There had been no rain, but rain could happen at any moment. That 'any moment' might be days away was irrelevant.
He came to the edge of the quarry and walked down into the streambed, stopping for a few seconds to see if he identified anything glinting in the water, or anything that deserved to be squashed. There was none of either. He'd recently found a big grey lizard and turned it into pulp, but that had been almost a week earlier, and the stream had offered almost nothing since.
The parking lot seemed to have little more on display. He peeked through the windows and windshields, well aware that these were cars he'd already glanced into several times in recent days. There was nothing in them. A mouse skittered away four rows down, a mouse that may have been a rat, but he didn't give it too much attention. He'd have no hope of finding it again, for those were swift little beasts.
Then he saw the doll. He didn't know if it was new there or if he'd missed it before, but it was an old Raggedy Ann doll propped up between the spare tire of an old green sedan and one of the columns that held up the overpass.
It was an odd position for the doll to be in, and he examined it curiously. It was almost as if somebody had held it in place while the car pulled in and pinned it there, but it didn't seem likely. He'd seen a lot of strange things in this city over the years, so it wouldn't really surprise him if that's what had transpired, but it would be a pointless thing to do with a toy.
Unless it had been a certain type of kid, he realized. Kids could be cruel. If this was another, smaller child's treasured toy, then yes, the torture of the doll would make perfect sense.
He tried to tug it out, but it was jammed in pretty hard. Perhaps if he pushed back on the car, if he tried to squeeze the tire... the doll came free.
Raggedy Ann's chest, arms, and head were clean, but the rest of her was a greasy mess. She looked to be in good shape otherwise, though, and he thought one of the old women on his street might be able to clean her. He was sure they'd dealt with worse, what with all the work uniforms and daily garments their husbands mucked up on their jobs.
What would he do with her then? A new doll, smiling like she was happily in the U.S.A. in the 1910s or 1920s?
He could use her as a gift. Or as a torture device. Or to scare some of the older people at the park. Or he could just destroy her. Make her his hate toy, stomp on her every time he felt like stomping on something nice.
He squeezed the doll between his backpack strap and his shoulder and went on his way. He was happy about his discovery and would seek no more fun out today, but would just go home; the critters of the parking lot and road should sing their praises to Raggedy Ann.
There was a rivulet of oily cold water running by the kerb in front of his house, and he looked at it in distaste. Maybe he could bathe Ann in that, get her all nice and uniformly disgusting before somebody cleaned her up. But that made no sense, not if he wanted her clean before he made any decisions about her. He stepped over the sludge and opened the door with the key he kept by his knife in his rear pocket.
The inside of the house smelled like old wood and musty potatoes, but he knew houses that exclusively smelled of age, houses without a trace of food in them. Those were ugly places, so he didn't mind this odor. His mother might've been somewhere in the kitchen or out back, but it didn't cross his mind to greet her. He hopped up the steps, went into his room, and locked the door.
He had a system that he used to keep his room relatively clean, and he followed that system religiously. In the small space immediately by the door he stripped down to his underwear, with even his socks going off, and hung everything from hooks he'd fashioned himself that hung from the wall and door. The backpack also went on the floor. Nothing that might have any dirt on it went in; he would've removed the underwear as well, but that would've made him feel a bit too vulnerable.
He'd found nothing of interest other than the doll today, so he didn't bother opening his backpack. It carried nothing of note. Taking Ann in his hands, he sat on the bed and now, feeling secure and with time on his side, examined her.
He confirmed his initial impression: other than the grease from the column and the tire, the doll was in great condition. There wasn't much wear to it, and all of its face was in great shape. Even the dress, which was greasy and stained, would be fine with a bit of cleaning; this was a new item, and it was a Treasure. His Treasure, too. He'd have it cleaned today.
Old Benda would help him. He trusted her because she often asked him favors, some important, some secret, some never spoken of, so he knew that she couldn't afford to screw him on anything. Benda would clean the doll, make it new again, and not charge him much. Or charge him a favor. Some of the favors he enjoyed. One of those favors had resulted in his love of squished critters.
He dressed up again and emptied the backpack into the corner. Then he put the doll in it and went back out to the street. As he exited he thought he heard his mother calling, but he paid no heed. He'd check on her later.
Benda lived several streets away, toward the more dilapidated part of the quarter. The men and women on the street seemed to pay him little heed but actually avoided him, and the only looks he had to deal with were from wisely distrustful cats who stood in corners or on empty barrels.
He came to her door and knocked twice, paused, then knocked thrice. Then he waited a moment and let himself in.
Benda was, as was increasingly the case, on her rocking chair by the long-inactive fireplace. He lightly kicked her foot and then gave her a slight shake of the shoulder, and she awoke. Not alarmed, but instead ready for anything this encounter might bring. She'd been through a lot, this woman, and she was still here to talk about it.
He pulled Ann from the backpack and showed her to the old woman.

[Author's notes: I think this one could develop in an interesting direction. The young man is a grey-area character, and I'd like to know more about his world. I'd like to know which way he ends up going, because his morality seems to stand on a knife-edge. Wrote this one at 8:30pm; this hour of the day does produce some interesting pieces.]
